            <h2 id="pattern-1"><span class="num">PATTERN 01</span>The REST-to-Action Pattern</h2>

            <p>This is the pattern everyone starts with, and for good reason. A REST API exposes resources through predictable URLs. You <code>GET</code> a resource, inspect the JSON, extract what you need, and trigger an action. It is simple, well-documented, and universally supported.</p>

            <p>The canonical form looks like this:</p>

            <pre><code>Trigger Event → GET /resource/{id} → Inspect Response → Take Action</code></pre>

            <p>The "action" could be anything: send an email, update a database, post to Slack, or — in the context of what we build at Stack & System — feed the data into an AI model and generate content.</p>

            <h3 id="p1-when">When to use it</h3>

            <p>Use the REST-to-Action pattern when:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>The API returns clean, predictable JSON</li>
                <li>You only need a small subset of the response fields</li>
                <li>The action is triggered by a discrete event (new order, new post, new review)</li>
                <li>You can tolerate a small delay between the event and the action</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Real-world examples: a webhook fires when a new product is added to your WooCommerce store. Your script <code>GET</code>s the product details, extracts the name, price, and features, and sends that data to an AI generator to write a product description. Or a new GitHub release is published. Your script fetches the release notes and generates a user-friendly newsletter entry.</p>

            <h3 id="p1-trap">The trap most people fall into</h3>

            <p>The trap is assuming the API response structure is a contract. It is not. APIs change. Fields get renamed. Nested objects get flattened. Arrays become objects. If your script hardcodes field paths like <code>data.product.name</code>, you are one API update away from a <code>KeyError</code>.</p>

            <p>The fix is defensive extraction. Always check if a field exists before using it. Always have a fallback value. Always log the raw response when something breaks, so you can compare it to what you expected. Better yet, validate the response against a schema before you touch any fields.</p>

            <p>Another trap: rate limits. REST APIs almost always have them. If your trigger event is bursty — say, 100 new products uploaded at once — your script will hit the limit on item 51 and fail silently for the rest. The fix is a rate limiter with exponential backoff, not a <code>time.sleep(1)</code> prayer.</p>

            <h2 id="pattern-2"><span class="num">PATTERN 02</span>The GraphQL Aggregation Pattern</h2>

            <p>REST APIs make you work for your data. If you need a user's profile, their recent posts, and their follower count, you make three requests. If you need that for 100 users, you make 300 requests. GraphQL fixes this by letting you describe exactly what you need in a single query.</p>

            <p>The pattern looks like this:</p>

            <pre><code>Define Data Requirements → Construct Query → POST to /graphql → 
Receive Exactly What You Asked For → Process</code></pre>

            <p>The power is in the "exactly." You specify the fields, the nesting depth, the relationships. The server returns a JSON object that mirrors your query structure. No over-fetching. No under-fetching. One request, one response.</p>

            <h3 id="p2-when">When to use it</h3>

            <p>Use the GraphQL Aggregation pattern when:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>You need data from multiple related resources</li>
                <li>Bandwidth or request count matters (mobile, serverless, high-frequency)</li>
                <li>The API provider offers a GraphQL endpoint with good documentation</li>
                <li>You want to reduce round-trip latency</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Real-world example: you are building a daily news brief. A REST approach would require separate requests for headlines, article bodies, author bios, and related tags. With GraphQL, you request all of it in one query:</p>

            <pre><code>query {
  articles(limit: 10) {
    title
    summary
    publishedAt
    author { name bio }
    tags { name }
  }
}</code></pre>

            <p>One POST. One response. Your automation script now has everything it needs to generate a curated newsletter without chaining five API calls together.</p>

            <h3 id="p2-trap">The N+1 problem in automation</h3>

            <p>GraphQL solves the client-side N+1 problem, but it can create a server-side one. If your query asks for nested data that the server resolves with separate database queries, you might be hammering the provider's backend without realising it. The provider will throttle you, and you will not know why.</p>

            <p>The fix is to profile your queries. Most GraphQL APIs expose query complexity scores or cost headers. Read them. If a query costs 50 points and your limit is 1000 per minute, you can run 20 of them. Plan accordingly. Cache aggressively. And never assume that "one request" means "one database hit" on the server side.</p>

            <h2 id="pattern-3"><span class="num">PATTERN 03</span>The Internal API Bridge Pattern</h2>

            <p>This is the pattern nobody talks about until they need it. You are pulling data from an external API — say, a product catalog or a news feed — and you want to process it before it hits your main system. But the external data is messy. It has fields you do not need. It uses different naming conventions. It might contain PII you are not allowed to store.</p>

            <p>The Internal API Bridge pattern solves this by inserting your own API between the external source and your consumer. Your bridge API receives the raw external data, sanitises it, transforms it to your internal schema, and exposes a clean endpoint for your automation scripts to consume.</p>

            <pre><code>External API → Your Bridge API (sanitize/transform) → 
Your Automation Scripts → AI / Database / Publisher</code></pre>

            <h3 id="p3-when">When to use it</h3>

            <p>Use the Internal API Bridge pattern when:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>The external API data is noisy, inconsistent, or poorly documented</li>
                <li>You need to enforce data governance (PII stripping, field validation)</li>
                <li>Multiple internal systems consume the same external data</li>
                <li>The external API changes frequently and you want to isolate that volatility</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Real-world example: you pull pricing data from three different affiliate networks. Each uses different currency formats, different product ID schemes, and different category taxonomies. Your bridge API normalises all three into a single schema — <code>product_id</code>, <code>price_gbp</code>, <code>category_slug</code> — and exposes <code>/api/v1/products</code>. Your automation script talks to your API, not three different ones. When Network B changes their response format, you fix it in the bridge, not in every consumer.</p>

            <h3 id="p3-trap">The schema drift problem</h3>

            <p>The trap is thinking your internal schema is permanent. It is not. As you add more external sources, your bridge schema grows. Fields become optional. Types become unions. What started as a clean <code>{ id, name, price }</code> becomes a monster with 47 optional fields and three levels of nesting.</p>

            <p>The fix is versioning. <code>/api/v1/products</code> stays stable. <code>/api/v2/products</code> gets the new fields. Your automation scripts pin to a version. When you need to change the schema, you release a new version and migrate consumers one at a time. Never break a running script with a schema change. That is how you get 3 AM pages.</p>

            <h2 id="pattern-4"><span class="num">PATTERN 04</span>The Partner API Handshake Pattern</h2>

            <p>Some APIs are not public. They are partner APIs, accessible only through OAuth2, mutual TLS, or signed requests. These are the APIs that hold the most valuable data — payment processors, shipping providers, ad networks, enterprise SaaS platforms — and they are also the most painful to integrate.</p>

            <p>The Partner API Handshake pattern is about managing the authentication lifecycle, not just the request. It is not enough to get a token. You need to refresh it before it expires. You need to handle consent revocation. You need to rotate credentials when the partner mandates it. You need to store tokens securely and never, ever commit them to version control.</p>

            <pre><code>Request Access → OAuth2 Handshake → Store Tokens Securely → 
Refresh Before Expiry → Make Authenticated Requests → 
Handle Revocation / Rotation</code></pre>

            <h3 id="p4-when">When to use it</h3>

            <p>Use the Partner API Handshake pattern when:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>The API requires OAuth2, mTLS, or request signing</li>
                <li>Token expiry is measured in hours, not years</li>
                <li>The partner has strict rate limits and usage policies</li>
                <li>The data is high-value and the integration is business-critical</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Real-world example: you are automating content generation for an e-commerce store that uses Shopify. Shopify's Admin API requires OAuth2 with a refresh token that expires every 24 hours. Your automation script cannot just store an API key in a config file. It needs to store the refresh token, detect when the access token is about to expire, request a new one, and update its stored credentials — all without human intervention.</p>

            <p>If you get this wrong, the script fails at 3 AM when the token expires. If you get it really wrong, you trigger the partner's security alert and they revoke your access entirely.</p>

            <h3 id="p4-trap">The credential rotation trap</h3>

            <p>The trap is hardcoding credentials or storing them in plain text. I have seen production scripts with API keys committed to GitHub, pasted into Slack, and shared in Google Docs. Do not do this. Use environment variables. Use a secrets manager. Use short-lived tokens with automatic rotation.</p>

            <p>Another trap: ignoring the partner's rate limit headers. Most OAuth2 APIs return <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> and <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code> in every response. Read them. Respect them. If you hit the limit, back off. If you get a 401, check if the token expired before you assume your credentials are wrong. Partners log every failed auth attempt. Too many failures and you are blacklisted.</p>

            <h2 id="pattern-5"><span class="num">PATTERN 05</span>The SOAP Legacy Wrapper Pattern</h2>

            <p>SOAP is not dead. It is just hiding in enterprise systems, government APIs, and legacy platforms that have been running since 2008. If you work in fintech, healthcare, logistics, or any industry with heavy regulation, you will encounter SOAP. And when you do, you need a pattern for dealing with it without losing your mind.</p>

            <p>The SOAP Legacy Wrapper pattern is about modernising the interface without modernising the backend. You accept that the SOAP service is not going away. You build a thin wrapper that speaks SOAP to the legacy system and exposes a clean, modern interface — usually REST or JSON — to your automation scripts.</p>

            <pre><code>Your Script → REST/JSON Wrapper → SOAP Client → Legacy SOAP Service → 
XML Response → Wrapper Parses → JSON Response → Your Script</code></pre>

            <h3 id="p5-when">When to use it</h3>

            <p>Use the SOAP Legacy Wrapper pattern when:</p>

            <ul>
                <li>The data you need is locked behind a SOAP service</li>
                <li>The SOAP service is stable, well-documented, and unlikely to change</li>
                <li>You need to integrate multiple legacy systems with different SOAP versions</li>
                <li>You want to isolate your automation scripts from XML and WSDL complexity</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Real-world example: you are pulling shipping data from a carrier that only offers a SOAP API. The WSDL is 4,000 lines. The responses contain 200 fields, 180 of which you do not need. Instead of parsing XML in every script, you build a wrapper service that calls the SOAP endpoint, extracts the five fields you care about — tracking number, status, location, estimated delivery, weight — and returns them as clean JSON. Your automation scripts never see XML.</p>

            <h3 id="p5-trap">The XML parsing nightmare</h3>

            <p>The trap is trying to parse SOAP XML with regex. Do not. Use a proper SOAP client library — <code>zeep</code> in Python, <code>soap-client</code> in Node — that handles the WSDL, the namespaces, and the type coercion for you. SOAP XML is verbose, deeply nested, and full of namespace prefixes that will break naive parsers.</p>

            <p>Another trap: assuming the SOAP service is as reliable as a REST API. Legacy SOAP services often have stricter uptime windows, longer response times, and less informative error messages. A <code>500 Internal Server Error</code> from a SOAP service usually means "something broke, good luck figuring out what." Build retry logic with exponential backoff. Log the full XML request and response on failure. And always have a fallback data source if the SOAP service goes down.</p>

            <h2 id="putting-it-together">Putting It Together</h2>

            <p>These five patterns are not mutually exclusive. A real automation system often uses two or three of them in sequence. You might pull data from a partner API using OAuth2 (Pattern 4), aggregate it with a GraphQL query (Pattern 2), sanitise it through your internal bridge API (Pattern 3), and trigger an action via a REST webhook (Pattern 1). If you are unlucky, one of those sources is a SOAP service (Pattern 5) that needs a wrapper.</p>

            <p>The key is knowing which pattern to apply where. Here is a quick decision matrix:</p>

            <pre><code>Simple resource fetch + action          → REST-to-Action
Multiple related resources, one query   → GraphQL Aggregation
External data needs sanitising          → Internal API Bridge
Partner/restricted API access           → Partner API Handshake
Legacy SOAP service                     → SOAP Legacy Wrapper</code></pre>

            <p>Each pattern has a failure mode. Each failure mode has a mitigation. The difference between a script that works on day one and a system that runs for years is whether you planned for the failures before they happened.</p>

